    20	thread_11_summary:Thread 11 = OPEN_POSITIVE_TARGETS.md §P11 — minimum number of straight lines required to cover all primes <= N under a chosen 2D embedding. Trivially 1 line on Z (primes collinear). Non-trivial in 2D embeddings: (a) Ulam spiral (1963) — diagonal lines correspond to Hardy-Littlewood quadratic-prime sequences f(n) = a*n^2 + b*n + c, dominated by Euler's n^2 + n + 41 and similar; (b) residue-class grid (n_x = n mod p, n_y = n div p), where each row is a prime residue class; (c) polynomial-image grids n -> (P(n), Q(n)) for P, Q polynomial. Quantity: minimum line cover number L(N) of the primes <= N in the chosen embedding. Cross-domain ingredient: Szemeredi-Trotter incidence theorem (1983), Erdos extremal incidence problems, Hardy-Littlewood prime k-tuple conjecture for quadratics. Question: does L(N) admit a polylog or sub-linear computable invariant? Lines of primes correspond to k-tuple-prime polynomial sequences; if covering achieves L(N) ~ pi(N)^alpha for some named alpha < 1, the invariant has algorithmic content for prime-density estimation.
    21	thread_11_a_grade_targets:(a) named-exponent L(N) scaling for one or more 2D embeddings (alpha < 1) with structural reason (HL k-tuple density of quadratic forms, or matched-baseline z-score >= 5sigma showing primes are non-trivially compressible in lines) — first explicit incidence-geometric prime-density theorem in the project; (b) polylog-time approximation of L(N) via line sampling that improves on naive O(pi(N)^2) brute force; (c) closed-form L(N) for a specific embedding (e.g., Ulam spiral) tied to Hardy-Littlewood constants for named quadratic forms.
    22	thread_11_slot_plan:
    23	  slot_1: build Ulam-spiral evaluator. Place primes <= N on Ulam spiral. Compute minimum line cover L(N) via greedy line-detection / LP relaxation at N in {10^4, 10^5, 10^6}. Compare to matched-baseline (random density-pi(N)/N points on the spiral) and HL prediction for the dominant prime-quadratic lines (Euler 41, Heegner 67, etc.).
    24	  slot_2: alternative 2D embeddings — residue-class grid (n mod p, n div p) for p = 2, 3, 5, 7, 11; polynomial-image grid (n, n^2 mod p). Compute L(N) for each. Cross-embedding scaling fit.
    25	  slot_3: theoretical shape — does L(N) follow Szemeredi-Trotter incidence bound L(N) >= pi(N)^{2/3} (random-points lower bound), or beat it via HL alignment? If beats, by what HL-related factor?
    26	  slot_4: algorithmic angle. Is there a polylog-time approximation algorithm for L(N) that exploits HL structure on prime quadratics?
    27	  slot_5: theoretical wrap. Either named-exponent scaling theorem with HL backing, or structural negative documenting why L(N) saturates at random-point Szemeredi-Trotter bound.
    28	recommended_next_action:Slot 4 of Thread 11. ALGORITHMIC ANGLE + scaling confirmation. Three concrete actions: (1) Push LP_primes(Ulam, N=10^6) — estimated runtime 30-60 minutes via scipy HiGHS sparse, ~10^6 candidate lines at K=5. Confirm or refute the 0.78 stability of LP_primes / LP_random at one more order of magnitude in N. If ratio stays at 0.78, slot 5 attempts a HL-backed proof of asymptotic constant. If drifts toward 1, the compression is asymptotically vanishing and thread closes B-NEGATIVE. (2) Try iterated rounding of the slot-3 fractional LP solution to a feasible integer cover — does it improve on greedy from L_greedy=989 (slot 1) toward LP_lower_bound = 0.78 * sqrt(10^6) = 780? Concrete approach: take fractional solution, round x_l > 0.5 to 1, repeat LP on uncovered primes. This would constitute a polylog-time approximation algorithm. (3) Inspect LP solution at N=10^5 for residue grids with q << sqrt(N) — does LP also tighten there or is wheel sieve genuinely integer-tight? Slot 3 closed only N=10^4 residue case; need to test whether the LP-vs-greedy gap is Ulam-specific or appears in other column-dominated embeddings. Cross-domain ingredient for slot 4: randomised rounding (Raghavan-Thompson 1987) and iterated rounding (Lavi-Swamy / Singh-Lau on LP-rounding for set-cover-like problems).
